[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Critical CSS, Code Splitting, and Delivery Optimization in CSS3"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"Modern web performance relies heavily on how efficiently styles and assets are delivered to the browser. As applications scale, monolithic stylesheets become performance bottlenecks, delaying the First Contentful Paint (FCP). This tutorial explores advanced techniques for optimizing CSS delivery using Critical CSS, code splitting strategies, and modern CSS3 features."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"Understanding Critical CSS"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"Critical CSS is the minimal set of CSS required to render the above-the-fold content of a web page. By inlining this critical subset directly into the HTML document head, the browser can render the initial view without waiting for external stylesheets to download and parse."},{"order":5,"type":"code","text":"<style>
  /* Above-the-fold critical styles */
  body {
    margin: 0;
    font-family: -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, "Segoe UI", Roboto, sans-serif;
    background-color: #f8f9fa;
    color: #333333;
  }
  header.hero {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    align-items: center;
    padding: 4rem 2rem;
    background: #ffffff;
  }
</style>"},{"order":6,"type":"subheading","text":"Asynchronous Loading of Non-Critical CSS"},{"order":7,"type":"paragraph","text":"Once the critical CSS is inlined, the remaining styles must be loaded asynchronously to prevent render-blocking. This is achieved using the rel="preload" technique combined with an onload event handler to switch the stylesheet to rel="stylesheet" once loaded."},{"order":8,"type":"code","text":"<link rel="preload" href="styles.css" as="style" onload="this.onload=null;this.rel='stylesheet'">
<noscript><link rel="stylesheet" href="styles.css"></noscript>"},{"order":9,"type":"subheading","text":"CSS Code Splitting with CSS3 and Bundlers"},{"order":10,"type":"paragraph","text":"Code splitting breaks down your global stylesheet into modular, route-specific, or component-specific chunks. Modern bundlers (like Webpack, Vite, or Rollup) automatically handle CSS extraction when utilizing dynamic imports in JavaScript or native CSS @import rules."},{"order":11,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Component-level splitting: Load styles only when the specific component mounts in the DOM."},{"order":12,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Route-based splitting: Load separate CSS bundles for dashboard, checkout, and landing pages."},{"order":13,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Conditional loading: Utilize media queries or CSS3 container queries to serve layout styles only when specific constraints are met."},{"order":14,"type":"subheading","text":"Leveraging CSS3 Variables for Dynamic Delivery"},{"order":15,"type":"paragraph","text":"CSS3 custom properties (variables) enable scoped theming and reduce stylesheet bloat by removing the need for redundant utility classes. By defining variables at the root level and overriding them conditionally, you minimize duplicated CSS rules."},{"order":16,"type":"code","text":":root {
  --primary-color: #007bff;
  --spacing-unit: 1rem;
}

[data-theme="dark"] {
  --primary-color: #bb86fc;
  --background-color: #121212;
}

.card {
  background-color: var(--background-color, #ffffff);
  padding: var(--spacing-unit);
  border-color: var(--primary-color);
}"},{"order":17,"type":"subheading","text":"Best Practices for Delivery Optimization"},{"order":18,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Minify and compress: Always serve your CSS via Gzip or Brotli compression alongside minification."},{"order":19,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Limit @import chains: Avoid deep chains of CSS @import statements, as they serialize network requests and block rendering."},{"order":20,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Automate extraction: Integrate tools like Critters or Critical into your CI/CD build pipeline to generate critical CSS dynamically."}]
